        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "a18ace56-d1cf-5405-959f-702e7dfeea59",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Cases: Slaughter-House Cases, Ward v. Maryland, United States v. Harris, United States v. Darby Lumber Co.\n- Concepts: Fourteenth Amendment, Privileges or Immunities Clause, privileges and immunities, Commerce Clause, freedom of movement, civil rights law, Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938\n\nFacts:\n- Ward v. Maryland, 79 U.S. 418 (1871), was cited as a case in which the Court held that authority under the privileges and immunities clause was given to the states.\n- The Slaughter-House Cases, 83 U.S. 36 (1873), was a pivotal early civil rights case.\n- The Slaughter-House Cases held that the Fourteenth Amendment protects the privileges or immunities of citizenship of the United States rather than the privileges and immunities of state citizenship.\n- United States v. Harris, 106 U.S. 629 (1883), was cited as another case in which the Court maintained that authority under the privileges and immunities clause was given to the states.\n- United States v. Darby Lumber Co., 312 U.S. 100 (1941), upheld the Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938.\n- United States v. Darby Lumber Co. held that Congress has power under the Commerce Clause to regulate employment conditions.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "537ff09c-1478-508f-987f-ad12ce753c6a",
        "text": "Page: Slaughter-House Cases. Sentence: It was a pivotal case in early civil rights law and held that the Fourteenth Amendment protects the privileges or immunities of citizenship of the United States, not privileges and immunities of citizenship of a state.\nPage: United States v. Darby Lumber Co.. Sentence: United States v. Darby Lumber Co., 312 U.S. 100 (1941) , was a case in which the United States Supreme Court upheld the Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938, holding that the U.S. Congress had the power under the Commerce Clause to regulate employment conditions.\nPage: Freedom of movement under United States law. Sentence: Under the \"privileges and immunities\" clause, this authority was given to the states, a position the Court held consistently through the years in cases such as \"Ward v. Maryland,\" 79 U.S. 418 (1871), the \"Slaughter-House Cases,\" 83 U.S. 36 (1873) and \"United States v. Harris,\" 106 U.S. 629 (1883).",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
